  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p">
    There are domains where one table per entity is enough. And there are domains
    where the rules do not fit into columns: they depend on context, change from
    one installation to another and affect several parts of the system at once.
    In those cases, DDD decides where each rule lives, and how many layers the
    business needs: the ones the complexity of the system and its integrations
    demand, and not one more.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2">The domain is not the database</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-2">
    The first symptom of a complex domain is that the data model starts deciding
    behaviour. Validations spread across the database, the controller and the
    interface, and nobody knows which one is the source of truth anymore.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-3">
    DDD proposes the opposite: model the behaviour and the language of the
    business, and treat persistence as a detail. Not because the database does
    not matter, but because the rules must be testable without it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-2">Shared language</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-4">
    A winery does not talk about “records” or “rows”: it talks about bottles,
    references, vintages, batches and positions. When the code uses that same
    vocabulary, conversations with the business and with hardware vendors become
    shorter.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-5">
    In Bodega Digital that vocabulary was part of the design: a bottle is an
    identifiable unit —vintage, variety, plot—, not a stock row; an RFID reader
    has capabilities, not just an identifier. The model reflects how the work is
    done.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-3">Boundaries: where the cost of change lives</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-6">
    The useful question for finding a bounded context is not “which entities go
    together?”, but “what changes together and what can change separately?”.
    Inventory rules change per winery; an RFID reader's protocol changes per
    manufacturer and model. They are two different speeds and deserve different
    boundaries.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-7">
    That is why each integration —RFID readers, computer vision, demand
    forecasting— stayed behind its own adapter, while the domain kept the rules
    that do not depend on hardware. Changing a vendor does not force changes in
    the domain; changing a business rule does not force changes in the adapters.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-4">Aggregates and identifiable units</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-8">
    An aggregate is a consistency boundary. In a wine inventory, the bottle is a
    good candidate: it has its own identity —vintage, variety, plot—, a position
    and a life cycle. Modelled that way, its transitions can be tested and errors
    stop being side effects.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-9">
    That boundary also prevents the giant aggregate that knows everything. The
    bottle does not need to know the whole inventory or the reader's protocol; it
    needs to know what it is, where it is and what state it is in.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-5">Domain events</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-10">
    When a bottle changes state, that change is a fact of the business. Publishing
    it as an event lets other modules react without the bottle knowing who they
    are. It is the same idea of boundaries, applied to time: the domain declares
    what happened and each consumer decides what to do.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-11">
    The discipline lies in not turning the event into a hidden call. If the sender
    needs a response to continue, it probably was not an event.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-6">The cost of DDD</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-12">
    DDD demands more conversation and more initial modelling than opening a table
    and moving on. In a simple domain, that cost is not recovered: a forms
    application with clear rules does not need aggregates or contexts.
  </p>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-13">
    It is worth it when there are several sources of variation —installations,
    vendors, rules that change— and when the cost of a badly placed change is
    high. There, modelling is what prevents every new rule from becoming one more
    conditional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ddd-dominios-complejos-h2-7">Closing</h2>
  <p id="ddd-dominios-complejos-p-14">
    DDD does not make a domain complex; it recognises that it already was and
    decides where to put each thing. The diagram may end up elegant and clear, but
    that is not the signal: the signal is that adding another implementation of a
    port or a new abstraction touches fewer files than it used to.
  </p>
